// Demonstrates cRust's core scoping rule (SPEC.md §3): assignment
// walks outward through enclosing recipe scopes and mutates an
// existing binding if it finds one, rather than needing a declare
// keyword to say which scope a variable belongs to. Three patterns
// that rule makes possible, each written out in prose in SPEC.md §3
// but not previously turned into a runnable file.

// Accumulator: `total` lives in sum()'s own scope, and the knead loop
// body (not a scope of its own) just keeps mutating it directly.
recipe sum(nums) {
    total = 0
    knead i in nums {
        total = total + i
    }
    serve total
}

deliver(sum([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]))   // 15

// Mutating a captured global: bump() has no `tally` of its own — every
// call walks out to the global scope and mutates the real one. No
// global/nonlocal keyword needed.
tally = 0

recipe bump() {
    tally = tally + 1
}

bump()
bump()
bump()
deliver(tally)   // 3

// Nested closures: makeCounter() returns a recipe that captures
// `count` from makeCounter's own (non-global) scope, not from the
// caller's — the classic closure test. Functions are first-class
// (SPEC.md §2), so returning one and calling it later is ordinary.
recipe makeCounter() {
    count = 0
    recipe increment() {
        count = count + 1
        serve count
    }
    serve increment
}

counter = makeCounter()
deliver(counter())   // 1
deliver(counter())   // 2
deliver(counter())   // 3

// A second, independent counter must not share state with the first —
// each call to makeCounter() creates its own `count`.
another = makeCounter()
deliver(another())   // 1
deliver(counter())   // 4
